/// AST Arena
///
/// A process-global bump arena for the compiler's own AST nodes.  Every
/// `new T(...)` whose `T` derives from `compiler::ast::node::ASTNode` is
/// lowered by `NewDeleteEmitter::emit_new` to call `cryo_ast_arena_alloc`
/// instead of `malloc`; the node's vtable seed and constructor run exactly
/// as with `malloc`, only the storage comes from the arena.  The whole region is
/// reclaimed in O(blocks) at each target boundary via `cryo_ast_arena_reset`
/// (called next to `reset_shared_type_cache` in `instance.cryo`).
///
/// Why this fixes the OOM: each `cryo build` compiles a `[lib]` then a
/// `[[bin]]` target, each with its own heap-leaked `CompilationContext`.
/// AST nodes are never individually freed, so the lib's parse trees +
/// monomorphized clones (~1.2 GB) survived into the bin build, summing the
/// peak to ~2.4 GB.  Resetting the arena between targets caps the peak at
/// the larger single target instead of the sum.
///
/// Soundness:
///   - Cryo compilation is single-threaded, so a module-level global needs
///     no synchronization - same argument as `g_shared_type_cache`
///     (codegen/type_map.cryo).
///   - The arena holds ONLY target-scoped AST nodes (everything that is
///     leaked-until-exit today).  Nothing that must outlive a target may be
///     arena-allocated.  `delete` of an arena node is lowered to a no-op
///     (see `emit_delete`); `free()` on arena memory would corrupt the heap.
///   - Determinism: allocation order is deterministic and the compiler is
///     pointer-value-independent (self-host is byte-identical run to run),
///     so routing allocation through the arena cannot change emitted IR.
///   - No bootstrap loop: the arena's own backing (`Arena`/`Chunk`/`Layout`
/// - plain structs, not `ASTNode`) comes from `intrinsics::malloc`, so
///     this module's allocations never route back into the arena.

namespace compiler::codegen::ast_arena;

import std::alloc::arena;       // Arena (+ inherent `bump`)

/// Process-wide AST arena.  Heap-boxed + leaked so module-global init is a
/// single null check; reset (not drop) reclaims it between targets.
mut g_ast_arena: Arena* = null;

/// Lazy-init accessor.  Idempotent; every call after the first returns the
/// same handle.  8 MiB chunks keep the chunk list short for the ~1 GB of AST
/// a self-host build produces (tunable).
function get_ast_arena() -> Arena* {
    if (g_ast_arena == null) {
        const _box: Box<Arena> = Box<Arena>::new(
            Arena::with_chunk_size(8 * 1024 * 1024));
        g_ast_arena = _box.leak();
        // Publish this region as THE per-target global arena so that, once
        // the driver activates routing (instance.cryo), the compiler's whole
        // `T[]`/`HashMap`/`Box`/`String` container heap shares this one arena
        // and its one reset.  Publishing alone is inert: `g_alloc_arena_active`
        // stays false until the driver brackets the heavy pipeline, so no
        // allocation routes here yet.  Free/realloc consult `owns()` on libc
        // pointers (never arena-resident) -> always false -> behavior unchanged.
        GlobalArena::publish(g_ast_arena);
    }
    return g_ast_arena;
}

/// Allocate `size` bytes aligned to `align` from the AST arena.  Called by
/// EMITTED code at every `new <ASTNode-derived>` site (resolved by qualified
/// name through the normal cross-module function resolver, exactly like the
/// constructor call a few lines below the malloc site in
/// new_delete_emitter.cryo).  Returns uninitialised storage; the caller seeds
/// the vtable and runs the constructor.  Returns null on OOM, matching
/// `malloc` so the existing downstream null-checks behave identically.
public function cryo_ast_arena_alloc(size: u64, align: u64) -> void* {
    mut a: Arena* = get_ast_arena();
    mut sz: u64 = size;
    if (sz == 0) { sz = 1; }       // malloc(0) is UB on some platforms
    mut al: u64 = align;
    if (al == 0) { al = 16; }      // malloc-parity alignment floor
    // Call the inherent raw bump directly (not the `Allocator` trait) so this
    // hot path doesn't depend on cross-module trait-impl resolution and needs
    // no `allocator`/`layout`/`result` imports.  Returns null on OOM, matching
    // `malloc` so existing downstream null-checks behave identically.
    return a.bump(sz, al);
}

/// Publish `a` as THE current AST + container arena, replacing whatever was
/// current before.  Both the AST-node path (`cryo_ast_arena_alloc` -> the
/// module global `g_ast_arena`) and the container path (`GlobalArena`) are
/// pointed at `a`, so every allocation this compile makes lands in `a`.
///
/// This exists for the LANGUAGE SERVER, which owns one arena PER open document
/// instead of a single process-global one.  A fresh compile of document B must
/// not disturb document A's already-built context, so the daemon gives each
/// session its own `Arena` and calls this to make the session-of-interest's
/// arena current before compiling OR reading that session's context.  Making
/// it current before a READ matters for correctness: `GlobalAlloc`'s free/
/// realloc route by `GlobalArena::holds(ptr)`, which only knows the currently
/// published arena - so the published arena must always own the pointers the
/// active operation might free, i.e. it must match the context being touched.
///
/// The CLI build path never calls this: it keeps the single lazily-created
/// `g_ast_arena` throughout, so this is inert for `cryo build`.
public function cryo_ast_arena_set_current(a: Arena*) -> void {
    g_ast_arena = a;
    // Point the container-heap router at the same region (see get_ast_arena).
    GlobalArena::publish(a);
}

/// Clear the current-arena slots, but only if `a` is the arena they hold.
///
/// A caller about to free its arena MUST retract first.  `GlobalArena` keeps a
/// raw pointer to the published arena and `holds()` dereferences it on EVERY
/// deallocation, so an arena that is freed while still published turns the next
/// `free` anywhere in the process into a wild read.
///
/// The identity guard matters once more than one arena is alive (the LSP keeps
/// one per open document): retracting an arena that is not the published one
/// would clear a different document's routing and send its arena-resident
/// buffers to libc `free`.
public function cryo_ast_arena_retract(a: Arena*) -> void {
    if (a == null || g_ast_arena != a) { return; }
    g_ast_arena = null;
    GlobalArena::publish(null);
    GlobalArena::set_active(false);
}

/// Enable/disable routing the compiler's **container** heap (every `T[]`,
/// default `HashMap`/`Box`/`String` - all the `GlobalAlloc`-backed storage)
/// into the per-target arena that already holds the AST nodes.  The driver
/// (`instance.cryo`) brackets this around the heavy pipeline: ON once a
/// target's cross-reset survivors (`output_path`/`stdlib_root` strings, the
/// leaked `ProjectConfig` clone) are already constructed; OFF before result
/// construction and before the next target's `cryo_ast_arena_reset`.  While
/// active, fresh allocations bump the arena and are bulk-reclaimed at the
/// next reset instead of leaking with the heap-leaked `CompilationContext` - 
/// this is what caps peak RSS near one target instead of (lib + bin).  AST
/// node allocation (`cryo_ast_arena_alloc`) is NOT gated by this flag; it
/// always uses the arena.  Free/realloc consult arena ownership regardless
/// of this flag, so toggling it OFF never strands a live arena pointer.
public function cryo_alloc_arena_set_active(active: boolean) -> void {
    GlobalArena::set_active(active);
}

/// Reclaim the entire AST arena.  Called by the driver at each target
/// boundary (after the previous target's compile - including linking - has
/// fully returned, so all of its AST nodes are dead).  No-op before the
/// first allocation.  `reset` keeps the chunks for reuse rather than freeing
/// them, amortizing the malloc/free round-trip across targets.
public function cryo_ast_arena_reset() -> void {
    // Publish + reset in one step.  Publishing the arena here (rather than
    // lazily on the first AST-node `new`) guarantees `GlobalArena` is live the
    // instant a caller flips routing on - so container allocations made BEFORE
    // the first `new` route into the arena too, instead of being pinned to libc
    // for their whole lifetime.  This matters for the LSP: `recompile()` sets
    // routing active then builds a fresh `CompilationContext`/`Resolver` (whose
    // `scopes`/intern tables and the discovery source buffers allocate before
    // any AST node exists); without an already-published arena those land in
    // libc and, because `GlobalAlloc::reallocate` keys on `holds(ptr)`, every
    // subsequent grow stays libc and leaks with the per-recompile ctx.
    // CLI-neutral: the build path keeps routing INACTIVE across discovery
    // (set_active stays false there), so publishing early changes nothing until
    // it activates at Frontend.
    mut a: Arena* = get_ast_arena();
    a.reset();
}

/// Free the entire AST arena's chunks back to the allocator (and, for the
/// large mmap-backed chunks, to the OS).  Unlike `cryo_ast_arena_reset`,
/// which keeps the chunks resident for reuse, this returns the memory so a
/// completed target's heap does not stay resident while the next target
/// builds.  The arena re-grows from empty on the next allocation.
public function cryo_ast_arena_release() -> void {
    // Publish (see `cryo_ast_arena_reset`) so a caller that flips routing on
    // right after this still routes into the arena, then free all chunks back
    // to the OS.  The LSP calls this per recompile instead of `reset`: `reset`
    // keeps chunks resident for reuse, but a long-lived daemon compiling a
    // fresh project every keystroke sees the per-compile high-water vary
    // slightly (edited source -> different sizes), which defeats chunk reuse and
    // leaks one chunk per recompile.  Releasing bounds peak at a single
    // compile's footprint; the re-`mmap` cost is trivial against an ~8 s LSP
    // compile.
    mut a: Arena* = get_ast_arena();
    a.release();
}
